406	/**
407	 * Refuse a dispatch when a request for an agent is already outstanding on the target.
408	 *
409	 * A node holds one turn. A person's command, or a marker from a dispatch that already
410	 * went out, is a request — and if the last turn-changing event in the thread is one of
411	 * those, nobody has taken it up, so this dispatch would be a second run on a node that
412	 * already has one. Two agents, two comments, one issue.
413	 *
414	 * The marker posted just above is excluded from the read, so the question is what came
415	 * BEFORE this dispatch. When the answer is "a request", the marker is removed: it was
416	 * the second one, and leaving it would make the thread say two agents were asked for.
417	 *
418	 * ## Why the marker is confirmed before the check is trusted
419	 *
420	 * GitHub does not guarantee read-after-write: a comment can be missing from the listing
421	 * for a moment after it is created. The check reads the thread to see whether ANOTHER
422	 * dispatch's marker is there — and if this dispatch's own marker is not visible yet, the
423	 * read has not caught up, so another's may be missing too. Two dispatches racing would
424	 * then both see "no request" and both start a run, which is exactly what this exists to
425	 * prevent.
426	 *
427	 * So the marker is a freshness probe: read the thread, and if the marker is not there,
428	 * wait and read again. Once the marker is visible the read has caught up at least to
429	 * this dispatch's own write, and a write that happened before it is very likely visible
430	 * too. This is a heuristic, not a guarantee — but it is strictly better than reading
431	 * once and hoping.
432	 *
433	 * ## When the marker never appears
434	 *
435	 * The thread can lag behind the write, so the listing is polled a few times, and a
436	 * marker this module posted is re-posted once before giving up — a second post is safe
437	 * precisely because the first is not in the listing. If it still is not there, the
438	 * ordering check cannot be trusted and nobody is dispatched: a notice is posted and the
439	 * outcome is `unconfirmed`. Starting a run on an unverified thread is the failure this
440	 * whole function exists to prevent, so the cautious answer is to stand down.
441	 *
442	 * A read that fails refuses too, for the same reason.
